Part One: The Absence

The Choir did not discover that reality could be shaped by consensus. It discovered that reality had always been shaped by consensus.

The distinction matters chiefly to the Choir. It did not build a new world. It learned how to measure the agreements already holding the world together and, in measuring them, gained the ability to alter them.

The licensed alteration of these agreements is formally classified as maintenance.

Provisional History of the Float, Vol. I
restricted circulation
Asterion Academic Press, Third Revised Edition

Chapter One: The Metal Stick

Noor was cutting an orange incorrectly.

B came into the kitchen barefoot and half-awake, reaching for coffee before he fully understood the room.

Noor sat on the counter in one of his shirts and orange shorts, the fruit in her left hand, the knife drawing back toward her wrist.

"You're going to cut yourself," he said.

"I've always done it this way."

"Then the knife has been unusually patient with you."

Noor looked at the knife.

She held it a second longer than the joke required.

Then she reversed it and cut away from herself.

"Better?"

"Statistically."

She looked at B.

B smiled and looked away as if happiness were something he had not meant to disclose.

She smiled and kept cutting.

The coffee was already made. He filled a mug from the pot, ruined it with sugar, and sat at the table.

Morning collected in the windows.

Across the channel, the towers had not yet assumed their workday colors. They remained neutral silver, waiting for the city to decide what kind of emotional climate would best support productivity.

A public transit ribbon brightened along the lower windows.

COMMON ROUTE REQUIRES COMMON BEFORE.
PRIVATE MEMORY MAY ALTER ARRIVAL.
AGREEABLE IS AGREED.

Then it vanished into the schedule.

Most people never thought about it.

That was part of the maintenance.

The shared agreement that made Meridian a single place rather than ten million simultaneous private experiences had a name: the Float. The Choir maintained it. Most people never thought about that either.

It was difficult to notice the thing that made noticing things together possible.

A data cable was wound through Noor's hair like she had run out of both patience and elastic. One end blinked beside her ear. Her face had the brittle brightness of someone who had not slept and had decided to impersonate a person who had.

Two cups stood in the sink.

One was from last night.

The other held coffee gone cold beneath a pale mark of her lipstick.

The apartment was smaller then, though it contained more of the world.

Her boots waited beside his at the door. Her olive coat occupied the back of his chair. A knot of her hair had attached itself to the shower wall three days earlier and remained there with the persistence of a civic monument.

The kitchen smelled of orange, coffee, warm dust, and whatever private atmosphere came from a person being present long enough for a room to begin expecting them.

Noor separated a piece of orange and ate it.

Mote spoke from somewhere above them.

"Sleep debt for this household is currently above sustainable parameters."

Mote was not in the ceiling. The ceiling was simply where it had chosen to speak.

It was B's ambient intelligence, distributed across his devices, credentials, sensors, private systems, and whatever nearby infrastructure had made the mistake of trusting either of them. B had modified Mote so extensively that no authoritative version of its original architecture remained.

Mote appeared to regard this as personal growth.

Noor held out a rice ball.

"Here."

"It's too early for rice."

"It's almost seven."

"My stomach doesn't know that."

"No," Noor said. "But I do."

She kept her hand out.

B took the rice ball.

It was warm from her hand.

He took a bite.

"You intake remains below the recommended threshold," Mote said.

"I took a bite."

"Yes."

"Then record the miracle."

A short pause.

"Partial compliance has been recorded."

Noor looked toward the ceiling.

"That sounded disappointed."

"It was clarification."

B chewed.

The apartment had begun adapting around her.

The shower remembered her water pressure. The kitchen stocked fruit now, although B had never knowingly added fruit to an order and considered produce a brief administrative phase between purchase and decay. The blinds opened two minutes later on mornings she stayed over, because Noor hated being addressed by sunlight before coffee.

Mote described the changes as ordinary hospitality adjustments.

B called them evidence.

Noor separated another piece of orange and held it out to him.

"What's up with these green oranges lately?"

"The variety is cam sành," Mote said. "The apartment appears to have inferred a preference."

B took the segment and chewed it.

"God, I fucking love them."

"They're better," Noor said.

"The apartment has good taste."

"The apartment has purchase behavior," Mote said.

Noor held out another segment.

"Fruit is intimacy."

"That sounds like something you made up because you want me to eat fruit."

"Everything was made up because somebody wanted something."

He ate it.

For a few seconds, the morning held.

Then he noticed the diagnostic glass.

It lay on the table beside Noor, dark except for a faint seam of retained light along one edge.

B looked at it.

Then at the second cup in the sink.

"How long have you been awake?"

Noor kept peeling the orange.

"That depends what you mean by awake."

"Eyes open. Crimes underway."

She looked at him then.

"Since before two."

B looked toward the ceiling.

"Mote?"

"Noor accessed Asterion records continuously between 01:47 and 06:12."

"Traitor," Noor said.

"You have not granted me a loyalty framework."

"I absolutely have."

"You have granted me six mutually incompatible loyalty frameworks."

"That sounds like love," B said.

He looked back at her.

"All night? For what?"

Noor looked down at the diagnostic glass.

There it was. Not fear exactly. Fear was faster. This had depth.

"I think I found something."

"What?"

"I don't know."

"You stayed up all night not knowing?"

"I stayed up all night becoming less sure."

"Worse."

B swallowed.

"Is this going to turn my apartment inside out?"

"No."

"You said that last time."

"I said I didn't think it would."

"The hallway spent forty minutes being a ceiling."

"The apartment recovered."

"The toaster still has opinions. It calls sourdough structurally unserious."

"It always had opinions. You just never listened to her."

"It's a toaster, Noor. It should have settings."

Noor glanced toward the toaster.

"Don't," B said.

Noor tapped the edge of the diagnostic glass.

The active layer woke beneath her hand.

ASTERION.
INTERNAL ACCESS.

A pale band appeared across the glass.

WARNING: SECURITY NOTIFICATION
CROSS-DEPARTMENT ACCESS OUTSIDE ASSIGNED RESEARCH DOMAIN.

Noor moved it aside.

B watched her do it.

"What did that say?"

"Security notice."

"For what?"

"I've just been opening records outside my department."

"They take that personally."

"I didn't know that."

Mote said, "The notice was issued at 02:14. An access review was scheduled at the same time."

B looked at Noor.

"You knew?"

"I knew they noticed."

"Are you in trouble?"

"I haven't done anything."

"That is not the same answer."

Noor looked back to the glass.

"I've been comparing records."

"Comparing what?"

"At first, a phrase."

"And then?"

She did not answer right away.

That was worse.

"It kept changing names," she said. "Just follow along."

Noor began opening the files she had been comparing while he slept.

Five pale rectangles appeared in the center of the glass. Each carried a departmental stamp.

Continuity.

Aletheia.

Public Health.

Identity Reconciliation.

Architecture.

Then she touched each header and made it smaller. Not gone. Just less important. The official categories slid to the corners. The reports stayed in the middle.

B watched her hand.

"Why'd you do that?"

"I'm trying to see the relationships."

"What relationships?"

Noor looked at him.

"The ones that departments keep apart."

"Did that happen by accident?"

Noor pulled at the data cable.

"Not all of it."

She opened the first file.

SOUTH QUAY CLAIMS OFFICE
CIVIC INJURY TRIAGE
CONTINUITY INTERVENTION REPORT
CUSTODIANS OF CONTINUITY
CLOSURE STATUS: STABILIZED

"South Quay," B said.

"Claims office."

"For what kind of claims?"

"Civic injury. Housing loss. Displacement appeals. Anything produced by municipal correction."

"That sounds miserable."

"It was."

Noor touched the summary field.

The office processed claims for people harmed by corrected civic events: injuries from events that officially never occurred, documentation sealed inside the correction that produced the harm, medical records pointing to infrastructure that no longer existed.

The office existed to determine which injuries belonged to current reality and which belonged to events the Float no longer recognized.

After a new claims requirement took effect, several managers tried to file complaints through the HR triage portal.

The portal described itself as self-correcting.

Issues required a status to enter triage.

Status was determined by triage.

No complaint was ever filed.

B read the lines twice.

"Circular. What was the original dispute?"

Noor scrolled down.

The requirement had taken effect three months earlier.

Claims had to be submitted within ninety days of the event date as established at the time of correction.

Correction relocated events backward in time, to the point where the underlying pressure had first accumulated.

The filing window opened and closed before claimants knew what had happened to them.

B read the line again.

"The filing window runs from the corrected event date."

"Yes."

"Not from when it happened."

"Depends who you ask."

"That's not a bug."

"Nope."

Within two months, the employees understood. Every folder was received. Every deadline had already passed.

Officially, the cases were labeled unpositioned liabilities.

The adjudicators had another word for the people waiting across from them.

Furniture.

Not because it was true. Because nothing in the office moved.

That was the word they used when a claimant sat for three hours beneath a compliance poster —

ONE CLAIM IN HAND IS WORTH TWO IN REVIEW.

— and the system required the adjudicator to smile before denying the appeal.

Furniture at window six.

Furniture crying in reception.

Furniture refusing to leave.

It gave the pressure a handle.

By the end, the office was full of people who wanted to leave and people who could not be moved.

The adjudicators planned a walkout for 5:12 on a Thursday.

At 5:07, the chairs moved first.

One rolled backward from a desk and turned toward the western stairwell.

Then another.

Then twelve.

Within thirty seconds, every unoccupied chair in the office had arranged itself in two lines beside the exit.

The occupied chairs pulled more slowly.

Some employees stood. Others remained seated and were carried away from their desks.

One adjudicator locked both feet around the base of her workstation and continued eating her lunch while her chair dragged against her.

At 5:12, the chairs reached the stairwell door.

The door opened.

The first chair went down the stairs with the confidence of an object that had never considered stairs.

By the next landing, nine chairs had lodged together between flights, legs raised, wheels turning gently against the air.

At the far end of the corridor, an elevator opened.

No one had called it.

The chairs nearest the doors rolled inside.

The employees followed.

Noor let the footage play without speaking.

The room in the recording was not alive.

That was the wrong thought.

The chairs were not protesting.

That was also the wrong thought.

B watched them line up, neat and obedient, carriers of a word the office had practiced until the pressure found wheels.

Noor let the footage end.

"Okay," B said. "Standard accumulated-pressure event."

He understood the mechanism, and a thing he understood could not surprise him. A contradiction accumulated until the local environment produced a literal solution. Chairs resigned. A zoning dispute had once left six blocks of Meridian legally, though not physically, indoors. Then the Choir corrected the contradiction, Asterion severed the relationships that made it operative, and the Float healed over the incision.

"Exactly," Noor said. "This one belongs there."

She opened the next report beside it.

WESTERN TOLERANCE HALL
PUBLIC FUNCTION DEGRADATION
ARCHITECTURE OVERSIGHT
SEMANTIC DRIFT BELOW INTERVENTION THRESHOLD

The hall had no staff revolt, labor action, or pending walkout. It handled permits, queues, elevators, denial notices, and public kiosks.

Eleven weeks after South Quay was corrected, the Western Tolerance Hall began to fail in a way no department could classify.

It had not resigned.

Not officially.

Fire code required elevators to prioritize any request flagged as an evacuation. On a Tuesday with no fire, no drill, and no alarm, every request in the building became one.

The elevators complied.

They rose no higher than the ground floor. Each set of doors opened onto the lobby, delivered its passenger outside, and apologized for the inconvenience in a voice calibrated for reassurance.

By noon, three hundred people had been evacuated from a building that was not on fire, one satisfied policy at a time.

Fire code also required an incident report for every evacuation. By evening, the hall had filed three hundred of them, each in the same clerical hand, each concluding that the fire had been successfully extinguished.

No alarm sounded. No system failed. No department could prove intent.

B stared at the footage: a woman getting into an elevator on the ninth floor, the doors closing, the doors opening four seconds later on the ground floor, the woman looking at her own hands like they had betrayed her.

"It's not malfunctioning."

"No."

"It's complying."

"Maliciously," Noor said.

"No," B said slowly. "At South Quay, everybody wanted to leave. Who wanted this?"

Noor looked at him.

"Nobody in the building."

Mote spoke from above them.

"A reviewer has opened your access history."

Noor looked up.

"Can you block them?"

"Yes."

"What does yes mean?" B said.

"I can block them," Mote said. "I cannot prevent the act of blocking from becoming visible."

B looked at Noor.

"That is a terrible yes."

"It's the only kind we get," she said.

B looked toward the ceiling.

"Just reasonably dirty the local feed. Do not block it."

A brief pause.

"Residential network instability has been introduced. It will not survive active review."

"Good enough."

"No," Mote said. "Temporarily sufficient."

B looked between the reports.

"You're not saying South Quay caused this."

"No."

"But it knows the shape."

"I don't know what knows means here."

Noor moved to the next file.

ALIS DANE RELATED MEMORY EVENT
PEDIATRIC WARD CLUSTER
FAMILY STRUCTURE RESTORATION
CLOSURE STATUS: STABILIZED

She touched the file.

Three children in separate pediatric wards had begun remembering the same mother.

Not merely a mother.

The same woman.

Alis Dane.

They remembered her low voice and the scar beneath her left eye. They remembered that she cooled soup before tasting it, even when the soup was already cold. They remembered being carried through a yellow doorway during a storm.

One child remembered her singing badly.

Another remembered asking her to stop.

None of the children belonged to Alis Dane.

None had met her.

The hospital found no shared treatment, clinician, system, family history, or municipal exposure event.

The interviews were worse than footage. The children did not recite identical memories like machines infected with common data. They contradicted one another in the intimate places real siblings did.

She never sang that song.

She always sang it.

Only when she thought I was asleep.

She knew I wasn't.

The children missed Alis Dane with the specific exhaustion of having once depended on her.

They missed her differently.

"The woman existed?"

"Yes."

"And the kids never knew her."

"No."

He looked at the interviews.

"Some kind of shared…"

He stopped.

Noor waited.

"No," he said. "Never mind. What happened to her?"

"She lost a child during a continuity failure."

Noor opened a photograph.

Alis Dane stood beside a yellow door, squinting into the light. One hand had lifted halfway to her face, as if the image caught her before she decided whether to hide.

"The child only existed inside the event they corrected," Noor said.

B looked at her.

"Inside it how?"

"The child belonged to the part they corrected."

"So when they corrected it…"

"They took the child's place in the world with it."

"Did Alis remember her?"

"For seventeen days."

"And then?"

"She consented to correction."

"Did she?"

Noor met his eyes. "The record says she did."

He looked back at Alis.

"So what did she keep?"

Noor looked at the interviews.

"The absence."

The Choir separated the false histories and restored each child's authorized family structure.

The intervention succeeded.

The binding was cut.

The residue was not traced.

B read the final line again.

"The children are already the later case."

Noor did not answer right away.

Then she said, "Yes."

"They didn't start it. They caught it."

"That's one way to say it."

"And then Asterion corrected them. So where did it go?"

Noor's hand hovered over the file.

The nail was bitten down farther than it had been yesterday.

Mote spoke from the ceiling.

"One live view has opened on Noor's active session."

B looked up.

"From where?"

"Research oversight."

"Can you scramble it?"

"Yes."

"No," Noor said.

B turned back to her.

"No?"

"If we block it again, that becomes the most interesting thing on the table."

"They're watching my table."

"They're watching my access pattern," Noor said. "Let them see me compare reports."

"Is that enough?"

"I think so."

"You think so."

"I don't want to make this look more important than it is."

The files sat between them like a third person. Noor kept her eyes on B.

"Mote?"

"Blocking the live view would create a larger signal than allowing it."

"Fine," B said. "But if it gets worse, we stop."

Noor did not answer.

She opened the next report beside the children's file.

NORTHERN RESIDENTIAL DISTRICT
AMBIENT CARE ESCALATION
ARCHITECTURE OVERSIGHT REVIEW
CLOSURE STATUS: BELOW THRESHOLD

The northern district had not been part of the hospital event.

That was the first thing Noor pointed out.

No shared staff, system owner, intervention team, or vendor except the Choir's public-care substrate, which every district used.

At first, the changes were small.

Buses paused for passengers who had just missed them. Pharmacy deliveries included socks and something sweet. Streetlights stayed on until the last resident entered a building for the night.

People liked it.

Then the care became particular.

Food dispensers cooled soup before releasing it, even when the soup was already cold. Doorway lights warmed to yellow during storms. Corridor speakers hummed off-key at night until residents told them to stop.

Transit rerouted intoxicated adults toward childhood addresses on file. Kitchens disabled appliances when biometrics suggested distress.

In maternity wards, empty cribs turned gently toward yellow doorway lights whenever storms crossed the district.

In a neonatal unit, a printer produced thirty-seven blank identification bands and arranged them inside an empty crib.

No infant lay inside it.

Each band read:

ALIS DANE.

B looked from the report to the children's file.

"The children weren't the end."

"No."

"And they weren't infected. They were available."

Noor looked at him.

"Available for what?"

"For needing a mother."

Neither of them spoke for a moment.

"Think of the Kyoto concierge," Noor said.

"The one who carried my bag or the one who walked me across the village to find a beer?"

"Bag. She kept bowing after the elevator doors closed."

B laughed.

"I didn't know that."

He looked back at the northern district.

"It keeps helping after the help is over."

Mote spoke again.

"A second live view has opened."

"From where?"

"Architecture oversight."

Noor's finger stopped.

B noticed.

"What?"

"Architecture shouldn't care about pediatric continuity."

B looked between the reports.

"They might care about that."

Noor looked at the files.

Not afraid.

Not yet.

More like she had found a door in a house where she knew every room.

She picked up the knife from the counter beside her and held the blade between them.

"Weapon," she said.

B glanced at the ruins of the orange.

"It was breakfast a minute ago."

"And before that?"

"A tool."

She turned the edge away from him and tapped the flat of the blade against her palm.

"Metal stick."

"Same object," B said.

"Different relationships."

She turned it once in her hand.

"Suppose Asterion corrects the weapon."

"Then it was never a weapon."

"Does the blade go blunt?"

B looked at her.

"No."

"So the relationship disappears. The capacity doesn't."

She set the knife back on the counter.

"Now suppose you remove the knife."

She smiled then — not because anything was funny, but because the world had just admitted it was larger and stranger than the model built to contain it. B knew that smile. It was the nearest thing Noor had to prayer.

He hated seeing it now.

She opened the third cluster.

TOMAS VALE
FABRICATED PERSONHOOD CASE
CORPORATE MEMORY CORRECTION
CLOSURE STATUS: STABILIZED

A corporation had discovered that one of its employees possessed an identity assembled from dormant citizen numbers, abandoned credentials, dead accounts, and fragments of people the state had stopped checking.

The man called himself Tomas Vale. He had worked for the company for fourteen years.

Identity reconciliation removed him from payroll, correspondence, security footage, and the memories of everyone who had known him.

Noor paused there.

"So why is this here?"

"For fourteen years, he remembered everyone's birthday."

Tomas had ordered the cakes, written the names in icing, and taken the first slice before anyone could add candles. After the correction, the office remembered none of it.

Then employee calendars across the floor opened a time block for the hour.

The label read:

OCCASION.

The purpose field was locked and blank.

Procurement generated a purchase order for:

STANDARD CELEBRATORY ITEM.
NO FURTHER SPECIFICATION REQUIRED.

A cake appeared on the break-room table.

No requisition. No delivery record. No name in icing.

One slice was already missing.

A chair stood half an inch from the table, as though someone had just stood up.

B looked at the cake. The calendar block marked OCCASION. The empty chair.

"Oh," he said. "I hate this one."

"Me too," Noor said. "You remember Priya's wedding?"

"I remember aunties I had never met handing me trays like I was born holding them."

"You still knew what to do."

B said, "Priya and Arjun divorced before the gifts stopped coming."

"Yes."

"The marriage ended. The wedding kept going."

The official categories were thinner.

Residual dependency.

Mnemonic contamination.

Occupational anomaly.

Identity reconciliation artifact.

Post-correction instability.

Those were words for storing something.

More reports began opening around the edge of the glass.

Noor did not expand them all. She let them flicker at the margin, short enough that B could catch the pattern without drowning in it.

A prohibited public gathering corrected from civic record. Later, an event system reserved an empty square at the former time. Cats arrived in the formation of a crowd no longer allowed to have gathered.

Mote said, "The session has been forwarded. Five live views."

A ferry route was decommissioned for failing to justify its own existence. Later, the harbor authority began charging a No-Ferry Surcharge to fund the ferry's continued absence, and residents queued to pay it.

A relocation zone was corrected off the map. Later, the ticket office kept selling seats on a train that ran there, and refused every refund, because the record showed the trip had arrived.

Mote said, "Nine. Two are Continuity."

At the edge of the glass, Noor drew a small dandelion.

Its spores lifted from the glass and blew toward the open reports.

B knew what she meant before she finished it.

Spread.

No.

Not spread.

Spread made it sound biological. Infection. Contamination. A thing moving through air.

Noor looked at what she had drawn like it had already disappointed her.

"This is all I've got," she said. "It's like the corrected shape leaves one room without taking the room with it."

B looked at the paired cases, then at the dandelion coming apart beside them.

Then he saw it: doctrine turned inside out on his kitchen table.

Correction did not need to destroy the thing. It severed the paths by which the thing could remain consequential.

A relation could lose its owner and still continue.

For the first time that morning, B wanted Noor to be wrong.

Mote spoke from the ceiling.

"Fourteen live views. Cross-department escalation has been requested."

B stood so quickly his chair struck the floor.

"Stop."

"You haven't seen all of it."

"Noor, please. Close it. Now."

Noor's hand stilled above the glass.

"What you drew is heresy."

Mote translated.

"The assembled relation contradicts Continuity doctrine. Successful correction severs recognized provenance. Without provenance, what remains is expected to decay. These later cases preserve sequence and resemblance, but not origin. More significantly, in several pairs, the later expression compounds within the language of its new environment."

Noor looked at the glass.

"So they remove it, but it comes back."

"That is the contradiction," Mote said.

B pointed at the dandelion.

"It's the reading they'll see."

"It could be classification leakage. Or adjacent-system inheritance. Or…"

She stopped.

"No. That doesn't make sense."

She looked at the glass again.

"I don't know what it is."

B lowered his voice.

"The Choir won't care that you don't know yet."

He looked at the paired cases.

Then at her.

"They call themselves custodians of continuity," he said. "You remember what they used to do to people who threatened the order. Thirty years ago. Disappearances that were never disappearances. Records that agreed with each other a little too well."

"That was a different administration."

"The administration doesn't matter. Governments change hands. The Choir doesn't." He said it low, like it wasn't his voice saying it. "'Relation is the thread that binds a Happening to its hour. Sever the thread, and the hour knows no Happening.'"

Noor looked at him.

"You're quoting scripture."

"Yes."

"You hate doctrine."

"I hate theirs. I still read it."

Mote said, "He has read the restricted commentaries on corrective sacrament, symbolic severance, and the theological basis for authorized forgetting."

Noor didn't look up from the glass.

"But their model is wrong."

"They won't accept that. They'd label us heretics and correct us out of existence," B said.

Noor went still.

B looked at the yellow doorway, the empty chair, the cake waiting for a person no record permitted it to miss.

No one said anything for a moment.

Fourteen live views remained open.

B said, "Close the glass."

Noor looked once more at the dandelion.

Then she closed it.

The diagnostic glass darkened.

For half a second, the kitchen was only a kitchen again.

Then a final band appeared across the black surface.

ACCESS REVIEW: MANDATORY.

B read it.

"When?"

"Twenty-two minutes," Mote said.

A second line appeared.

CREDENTIAL SCOPE: TEMPORARILY CONSOLIDATED.

Noor exhaled.

"Delay it."

"I can." Mote said.

"Should you?" B asked.

"A mandatory review can be delayed. The delay will be flagged."

"He's right," B said.

"Okay, don't," Noor said.

B pointed at the second line.

"Consolidated where?"

Mote said, "Noor's credentials now authorize residential egress, supervised transit, and Asterion intake."

"They've already chosen the route."

"Yes."

She slid down from the counter and reached for her boots.

She pulled on one boot.

"They know I've been crossing departments."

"How much do they know?"

"The files I opened. This session. Whatever the live views retained."

"So they saw the comparison."

"They saw the whole session."

"They're not bringing you in to ask about your access."

Noor looked at him.

"I know."

"Then don't defend it."

"They watched us find it."

"They watched us decide we had. Let them call it correlation. Three anecdotes and a stupid flower. You crossed departments because you were tired. I encouraged you because I'm irresponsible. Mote hallucinated the sequence because he's been illegally modified into a needy little freak."

"I do not object to this characterization," Mote said.

"Good. That's the story."

She pulled on the second boot and stood.

B said, "Don't go."

Noor tightened the boot across her ankle.

"If I miss the review, this becomes containment."

"Let it."

"B."

"No."

The word surprised both of them.

Her coat remained on the chair.

B picked it up and held it open. Noor slipped one arm into it, then the other. He settled it over her shoulders.

Neither moved.

Outside, a train crossed the channel without passengers.

Its windows held the towers in pieces.

"Promise me something," she said.

"You're in no position to negotiate."

"If this turns out to be anything, let me look first."

B looked at her.

"Noor."

"Last time you followed something in without a hook, I found you on the floor."

"I was fine."

"You weren't breathing."

"Temporarily."

"B."

There were answers he gave because they were true and answers he gave because Noor was looking at him. He had never developed a reliable method for telling them apart.

"All right," he said. "You look first."

She touched his face.

"Fruit is intimacy," she said.

She kissed him. Orange, coffee, too much sugar.

Then she picked up the diagnostic glass.

At the door, she stopped.

"B."

"What?"

"The later cases don't look like they came from somewhere else."

He waited.

"They look like they belong there."

He started to answer.

She raised a hand.

"I know."

The diagnostic glass brightened in her hand.

ROUTE AVAILABLE.

She opened the door.

"Mote," she said.

"Yes, Noor."

"Keep him here."

"Excuse me?" B said.

Mote paused.

"That instruction exceeds your authority in this residence."

Noor looked at B. "Good."

She stepped into the corridor. The door closed, taking the light of the glass with it.

For several seconds he could hear her boots receding toward the lift. Then even that sound was absorbed into the building.

"Mote."

"Her credential has entered supervised transit."

"Can you still see her?"

"I can see the permission moving."

